Lab Objective

This lab demonstrates how to retrieve multiple values from a database when the original query returns only a single text column. The attack uses a UNION-based SQL Injection combined with string concatenation to extract usernames and passwords from the users table.

Skills Practiced

UNION-based SQL Injection
Determining the number of returned columns
Identifying text-compatible columns
String concatenation
Retrieving data from another table
Understanding database-specific concatenation syntax

Methodology

Intercepted the product category request using Burp Suite Repeater.
Determined the number of columns using the ORDER BY technique.
Identified the column that accepts string data using NULL and a string value.
Used a UNION SELECT statement to retrieve data from the users table.
Combined the username and password values using string concatenation.
Retrieved the administrator credentials.
Logged in as the administrator to successfully solve the lab.

Key Concepts Learned

A UNION SELECT statement must return the same number of columns as the original query.
When only one output column is available, multiple values can be combined using string concatenation.
Different database management systems use different concatenation syntax.
A separator (such as ~) improves readability of concatenated values.

